-- ALAO prewarm bundle (idea I-063).  One script, no game file replaced.
--
-- WHAT IS SLOW, and why prewarming is the right shape of fix
--
-- The I-058 hitch profiler (attended run 20260920-125528-I-058-f5edb8, four
-- captures) says the worst single calls of ordinary player actions are:
--
--   ActorMenu_on_before_init_mode#ui_inventory.script:93   11.4 / 10.1 / 18.5 / 8.4 ms
--       and in EVERY capture that worst call was the FIRST inventory open of
--       the session; every later open in the same session is 3.2-6.4 ms.
--   actor_on_leave_dialog#ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab.script:407  9.5 / 7.0 / 6.5 / 6.4 ms
--       one call per capture, the one where an article was unlocked.
--   actor_on_jump#eft_jump_sounds.script:71   3.5 / 3.1 / 0.9 / 0.9 ms
--   actor_on_land#eft_jump_sounds.script:89   3.0 / 1.2 / 0.8 / 1.2 ms
--   actor_on_footstep#footstep_sounds.script:87  4.5 / 2.7 / 0.9 / 0.8 ms
--
-- Reading the three sources, all five are the same bug in three costumes: a
-- resource that is built lazily, on the frame the player asked for something.
--
--   * `ui_inventory.start()` does `if (not GUI) then GUI = UIInventory() end`.
--     `UIInventory:__init` runs `InitControls`, which is one
--     `CScriptXmlInit():ParseFile("ui_inventory.xml")` plus several hundred
--     widget constructions and six `utils_ui.UICellContainer`s.  `GUI` is only
--     dropped again in `UIInventory:actor_on_net_destroy`, i.e. on a level
--     change, so this is once per level load, on the frame you pressed I.
--   * `ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab.set_article()` starts with `local guide =
--     get_ui()`, and `get_ui` is `SINGLETON = SINGLETON or
--     pda_encyclopedia_tab()`.  That constructor parses `pda_encyclopedia.xml`
--     and runs `InitCategories()` over every encyclopedia category.  The first
--     article unlock of the session pays for it.
--   * `eft_jump_sounds` and `footstep_sounds` build a FRESH `sound_object(path)`
--     on every single call and throw it away.  Since the Lua side keeps
--     nothing, the only thing that can explain "first ~4 ms, later under 1 ms"
--     is engine-side state keyed by the sound's path.  Constructing each path
--     once is therefore enough to warm it; the scripts keep doing exactly what
--     they did.
--
-- WHAT THIS MOD DOES
--
-- All of it at `actor_on_first_update` - frame 7 of the level load, with the
-- loading screen still up; the profiler puts `on_loading_screen_key_prompt` at
-- frame ~62, so there are ~55 frames of screen left:
--
--   1. build `ui_inventory.GUI` if it is nil,
--   2. call `ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab.get_ui()`,
--   3. construct every sound path those three sound scripts can ever ask for.
--
-- WHY THE SLICER IS OFF (measured, 20260920-165259-I-063-953526)
--
-- Version 1.0 time-sliced step 3 onto `actor_on_update` with a 0.5 ms/frame
-- construction budget.  That was wrong, and the attended run said so: in the
-- cold capture, `actor_on_update#zzz_alao_prewarm.script` had 109 slices over
-- the 0.1 ms floor - 37 frames at 1.6-3.2 ms, 27 at 3.2-6.4, 3 at 6.4-12.8,
-- worst 17.7 ms - spread over ~110 frames from frame 7, so about half of them
-- landed AFTER the key prompt, in the player's face.  Blind to the arm order,
-- the player called that capture the worst of the four.
--
-- The reason is simple and it kills the whole idea of a time budget here: ONE
-- cold `sound_object(path)` costs 2-18 ms.  A budget can only decide whether to
-- start another item, never how long the one it starts will take, so a 0.5 ms
-- budget does not bound a slice - it just guarantees exactly one cold load per
-- frame, for a hundred frames.  Smaller budget, same hitches, more of them.
--
-- So step 3 runs synchronously too.  It costs ~250-300 ms on a cold OS file
-- cache and near zero once warm, on an `actor_on_first_update` that already
-- costs ~1.3 s behind a loading screen nobody is looking at.  The measured
-- first_update row went 43.9 -> 44.7 ms for the mod's own listener in the
-- sliced build; folding the queue in moves the rest there too.
--
-- `SLICE_SOUNDS` keeps the old path as an escape hatch.  It defaults off and
-- should stay off.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
--
-- It replaces no file and patches no function.  It calls two public entry
-- points of two other mods and constructs sound objects.  Disabling it changes
-- nothing except when the work happens.
--
-- BEHAVIOURAL NON-IDENTITIES, stated plainly (see README.md for the long form)
--
--   a) `UIInventory:__init` ends with twelve `RegisterScriptCallback(...,self)`
--      lines.  Building GUI early means those twelve listeners exist from the
--      loading screen instead of from the first inventory open.  Three of them
--      (`actor_item_to_ruck`, `actor_item_to_slot`, `actor_on_item_drop`) do
--      work OUTSIDE their `if self:IsShown()` guard - the "unequip artefacts /
--      helmet / backpack when the outfit goes away" rules - and two of them
--      (`npc_on_use`, `physic_object_on_use_callback`) call `start("loot", obj)`.
--      So with this mod the FIRST corpse you loot and the FIRST outfit you take
--      off in a session behave the way the second one already did.  That is the
--      mod author's steady-state behaviour, reached one event earlier.
--   b) The sound objects are kept alive in a table and NEVER played.  Nothing
--      reads them; they exist only so the engine keeps the resource.
--   c) `get_ui()` builds the encyclopedia tab with the article set as of load.
--      Every later `set_article` calls `guide:InitCategories()`, which rebuilds
--      the list from scratch, so nothing goes stale.
--
-- WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED OFFLINE
--
--   * That the engine's sound cost really is path-keyed and survives with the
--     object merely held (the Lua side gives no way to ask).  The evidence is
--     the profile above; the in-game A/B decides it.
--   * The size of each win.  These are engine-side costs; there is no honest
--     microbenchmark for them.  What is countable is what moves: one
--     `UIInventory()`, one `pda_encyclopedia_tab()` and N `sound_object()`
--     constructions, out of play and into the loading screen.

local VERSION = "1.2"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- configuration
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

local PREWARM_INVENTORY    = true
local PREWARM_ENCYCLOPEDIA = true
local PREWARM_SOUNDS       = true

-- v1.2, TARGET 1.  Grow the actor_bag cell pool behind the loading screen.
--
-- The v1.0 inventory prewarm was a no-op on GAMMA, because FDDA Redone
-- (custom_functor_autoinject.script:435) and SortingPlus
-- (zzz_rax_sortingplus_mcm.script:108) both build ui_inventory.GUI at
-- actor_on_first_update already.  But they build the OBJECT with an EMPTY cell
-- pool, and the per-call trace of the walk-out run 20260920-185607-I-062-a1c78b
-- shows that the pool is the actual cost:
--
--   open 1   cells 0 -> 19, grid 0 -> 7    17.4 / 9.2 / 7.9 / 12.0 ms
--   every later open, cells 19 -> 19, grid 7 -> 7    3.5 - 4.9 ms
--
-- So grow the pool at first update instead.  See the README for why this fires
-- no callback anybody listens to.
local PREWARM_INV_POOL     = true
-- Spare empty cells to build beyond what the ruck needs right now, so the first
-- few loots do not have to grow the pool either.  Each one is one UICellItem,
-- i.e. four xml:InitStatic calls, paid at frame 7.  0 disables headroom.
local POOL_HEADROOM        = 12

-- v1.2, TARGET 2.  Warm the engine's tutorial sequencer.
--
-- bind_campfire.script:176 (G.A.M.M.A. Cooking Overhaul) calls
-- game.start_tutorial("tutorial_campfire_ignite" / "_extinguish") when the actor
-- walks near a campfire.  In the walk-out run that listener had EXACTLY ONE call
-- of 705 / 716 ms per capture - a 786 ms frame, which the player felt - and
-- every other call was under 0.8 ms.  One call per session, whichever tutorial
-- came first, is the signature of a one-time engine cost: the tutorial
-- sequencer parsing ui\game_tutorials.xml and loading its UI.
--
-- So start and immediately stop a tutorial at first update, behind the loading
-- screen.  TUTORIAL_NAME is the no-op sequence this mod injects into
-- game_tutorials.xml through DXML (modxml_zzz_alao_prewarm_tutorial.script);
-- point it at an existing tutorial only if that injection is unavailable.
local PREWARM_TUTORIAL     = true
local TUTORIAL_NAME        = "alao_prewarm_noop"

-- Time-slice the sound queue onto actor_on_update instead of building it in
-- one go at first update.  DEFAULT OFF, and it should stay off: see the
-- "why the slicer is off" note above.  It is kept only as an escape hatch for
-- an install where a ~300 ms first_update is somehow a problem.
local SLICE_SOUNDS         = false

-- Only used when SLICE_SOUNDS is on.
local BUDGET_MS            = 0.5
-- If the timer cannot be calibrated, fall back to this many items per frame.
local FALLBACK_BATCH       = 6
-- Never slice for longer than this many frames, whatever happens.
local MAX_SLICE_FRAMES     = 1200
-- How long to spend calibrating profile_timer against os.clock, milliseconds.
local CALIB_MS             = 40

local VERBOSE              = true

-- `jump_sound = sound_object("jump\\jump_" .. material .. math.random(5))` and
-- `land_sound = sound_object("landing\\landing_" .. material .. math.random(3))`
-- in eft_jump_sounds.script.  The 5 and the 3 are literals in that file, so
-- they are literals here; everything else is read out of the live mod's tables.
local JUMP_VARIANTS = 5
local LAND_VARIANTS = 3

-- footstep_sounds.script and eft_jump_sounds.script both index a file-local
-- `exo_classes` that this mod cannot reach.  These are the values it can hold
-- on the shipped mods ("light" / "medium"), and every construction is pcall'd,
-- so a path that does not exist on someone's install costs one failed call.
local EXO_CLASSES = {"light", "medium"}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- state
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

local timer          = nil
local units_per_ms   = nil
local budget_units   = nil

local queue          = {}     -- sound paths still to construct
local qi             = 1
local warm           = {}     -- constructed sound_objects, held so the engine keeps them
local failed         = 0
local slicing        = false
local slice_frames   = 0
local sounds_done    = false

local report         = {}     -- one line per thing done, printed when we finish

local function note(fmt, ...)
	report[#report + 1] = strformat and strformat(fmt, ...) or string.format(fmt, ...)
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- timer
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Same trick as the I-048 profiler: profile_timer's units are not documented,
-- so measure them against os.clock instead of assuming.  time_global() is no
-- use here - it only advances once per frame.
local function calibrate()
	if not profile_timer then return nil end
	local t = profile_timer()
	local c0 = os.clock()
	local c1 = c0
	t:start()
	while (c1 - c0) * 1000 < CALIB_MS do
		c1 = os.clock()
	end
	t:stop()
	local ms = (c1 - c0) * 1000
	local u = t:time()
	if ms <= 0 or u <= 0 then return nil end
	return u / ms
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the sound path set
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

local function distinct_values(t)
	local seen, out = {}, {}
	if type(t) ~= "table" then return out end
	for _, v in pairs(t) do
		if type(v) == "string" and not seen[v] then
			seen[v] = true
			out[#out + 1] = v
		end
	end
	return out
end

local function push(path)
	queue[#queue + 1] = path
end

-- Built from the LIVE tables of whichever copy of those scripts won the load
-- order, so this follows the mod rather than a hardcoded list.
local function build_sound_queue()
	local ej = rawget(_G, "eft_jump_sounds") or eft_jump_sounds
	if type(ej) == "table" and type(ej.available_materials) == "table" then
		local mats = distinct_values(ej.available_materials)
		for _, m in ipairs(mats) do
			for i = 1, JUMP_VARIANTS do push("jump\\jump_" .. m .. i) end
			for i = 1, LAND_VARIANTS do push("landing\\landing_" .. m .. i) end
		end
		note("jump/land: %s materials", #mats)
		for _, c in ipairs(EXO_CLASSES) do
			push("exo-gear-walk-jump-land\\servo_exo_jump_" .. c)
			push("exo-gear-walk-jump-land\\servo_exo_land_" .. c)
		end
	else
		note("jump/land: eft_jump_sounds.available_materials missing, skipped")
	end

	local fs = rawget(_G, "footstep_sounds") or footstep_sounds
	if type(fs) == "table" and type(fs.limit) == "table" then
		local lim = fs.limit

		-- `sound_object("footstep\\" .. move_state .. material .. n)`.
		-- move_state is always "n_" in the shipped file (every other entry of
		-- `limit` is commented out), and water_ is handled by the engine.
		local n_lim = lim["n_"]
		if n_lim and type(fs.available_materials) == "table" then
			local mats = distinct_values(fs.available_materials)
			local cnt = 0
			for _, m in ipairs(mats) do
				if m ~= "water_" then
					cnt = cnt + 1
					for i = 1, n_lim do push("footstep\\n_" .. m .. i) end
				end
			end
			note("footsteps: %s materials x %s samples", cnt, n_lim)
		end

		-- cloth\cloth_N and gear_rattle\gear_rattle_N: in `limit` the keys that
		-- already carry their own folder are exactly the `extra_sounds` ones.
		for k, v in pairs(lim) do
			if type(k) == "string" and string.find(k, "\\", 1, true) then
				for i = 1, v do push(k .. i) end
			end
		end

		-- ladder\<material><part><N>
		if type(fs.ladder_materials) == "table" then
			for _, m in ipairs(distinct_values(fs.ladder_materials)) do
				for _, part in ipairs({"foot-0", "hand-0"}) do
					local class = m .. part
					local l = lim[class]
					if l then
						for i = 1, l do push("ladder\\" .. class .. i) end
					end
				end
			end
		end

		for _, c in ipairs(EXO_CLASSES) do
			push("exo-gear-walk-jump-land\\servo_exo_walk_" .. c .. "_1")
			push("exo-gear-walk-jump-land\\servo_exo_walk_" .. c .. "_2")
		end
	else
		note("footsteps: footstep_sounds.limit missing, skipped")
	end
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the indivisible prewarms
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

local function prewarm_inventory()
	local m = rawget(_G, "ui_inventory") or ui_inventory
	if type(m) ~= "table" then return note("inventory: no ui_inventory module") end
	if m.GUI then return note("inventory: GUI already built, nothing to do") end
	if type(m.UIInventory) ~= "function" and type(m.UIInventory) ~= "table" then
		return note("inventory: no UIInventory class")
	end
	local ok, gui = pcall(m.UIInventory)
	if ok and gui then
		m.GUI = gui
		note("inventory: UIInventory() built")
	else
		note("inventory: UIInventory() FAILED: %s", tostring(gui))
	end
end

-- TARGET 1: grow CC["actor_bag"]'s cell pool and grid to cover the ruck.
--
-- Only actor_bag is lazy.  `IMode_ResetInventories` is one line -
-- `self.CC["actor_bag"]:Reinit(self:ParseInventory(db.actor))` - and it is the
-- ONLY Reinit an "inventory" mode open performs; actor_equ, actor_belt and
-- actor_quick get their cells in InitControls via AddItemManual, so they are
-- already built by whoever built the GUI, and npc_*/actor_trade_* belong to the
-- loot/trade modes.
--
-- ignore_kind = true, the fourth argument, bypasses the sorter's kind filter, so
-- the pool ends up covering the whole ruck rather than whichever sort tab is
-- selected - a superset of any first open.  That matters because the sorter
-- state is not set up yet at first update.
local function prewarm_inv_pool()
	local m = rawget(_G, "ui_inventory") or ui_inventory
	local gui = type(m) == "table" and m.GUI
	local cc = gui and gui.CC and gui.CC["actor_bag"]
	if not cc then return note("inv pool: no GUI.CC.actor_bag") end
	if not (db and db.actor) then return note("inv pool: no db.actor") end

	local before = 0
	for _ in pairs(cc.cell or {}) do before = before + 1 end
	if before > 0 then
		return note("inv pool: already %s cells, nothing to do", before)
	end

	-- UICellContainer:Callback dispatches ONLY to self.owner[func], and the
	-- owner is this UIInventory.  The one subscriber, UIInventory:On_CC_Add,
	-- just sets self.update_info = true.  Suppressing it anyway costs nothing
	-- and is the mod's own documented mechanism - ui_inventory.script uses it
	-- for actor_equ, actor_belt, actor_quick and picker.
	local dc = cc.disable_callback
	local had_add, had_rm
	if dc then
		had_add, had_rm = dc["On_CC_Add"], dc["On_CC_Remove"]
		dc["On_CC_Add"], dc["On_CC_Remove"] = true, true
	end

	local ok, err = pcall(function()
		cc:Reinit(gui:ParseInventory(db.actor, nil, nil, true))
	end)

	local grown, rows = 0, 0
	for _ in pairs(cc.cell or {}) do grown = grown + 1 end
	for _ in pairs(cc.grid or {}) do rows = rows + 1 end

	-- Headroom: spare empty cells, built exactly the way AddItemInCell builds
	-- them (non-manual, so they use the container's own xml path).  A cell that
	-- was never :Set has no area, and UICellItem:Reset handles that - it falls
	-- through to Show(false) instead of FreeRoom.
	local extra = 0
	if ok and POOL_HEADROOM > 0 and utils_ui and utils_ui.UICellItem then
		local base = cc.idxer or grown
		for i = 1, POOL_HEADROOM do
			local idx = base + i
			if not cc.cell[idx] then
				local ok2 = pcall(function()
					cc.cell[idx] = utils_ui.UICellItem(cc, cc.st, idx)
				end)
				if ok2 then extra = extra + 1 else break end
			end
		end
	end

	-- Leave nothing visible behind.  Reset() clears the index tables, frees the
	-- grid and hides every cell but KEEPS the cells - which is the whole point,
	-- and is exactly what the real open's Reinit does to them first anyway.
	pcall(function() cc:Reset() end)

	if dc then
		dc["On_CC_Add"], dc["On_CC_Remove"] = had_add, had_rm
	end

	if ok then
		note("inv pool: %s cells + %s headroom, %s grid rows", grown, extra, rows)
	else
		note("inv pool: FAILED: %s", tostring(err))
	end
end

-- TARGET 2: warm the engine's tutorial sequencer.
local function prewarm_tutorial()
	if not (game and game.start_tutorial and game.stop_tutorial
	        and game.has_active_tutorial) then
		return note("tutorial: engine has no tutorial api")
	end
	if game.has_active_tutorial() then
		return note("tutorial: one is already active, skipped")
	end
	local ok, err = pcall(game.start_tutorial, TUTORIAL_NAME)
	if not ok then
		return note("tutorial: start_tutorial(%s) FAILED: %s", TUTORIAL_NAME, tostring(err))
	end
	-- Did the sequence actually exist?  This is the only way to find out, and
	-- it is why the line is worth printing: offline nothing can tell us whether
	-- the injected node parsed.
	local started = game.has_active_tutorial()
	pcall(game.stop_tutorial)
	local still = game.has_active_tutorial()
	note("tutorial: %s started=%s stopped=%s", TUTORIAL_NAME,
	     tostring(started), tostring(not still))
end

local function prewarm_encyclopedia()
	local m = rawget(_G, "ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab") or ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab
	if type(m) ~= "table" or type(m.get_ui) ~= "function" then
		return note("encyclopedia: no ui_pda_encyclopedia_tab.get_ui")
	end
	local ok, err = pcall(m.get_ui)
	note("encyclopedia: get_ui() %s", ok and "built" or ("FAILED: " .. tostring(err)))
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- the sound prewarm
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Build the whole queue now.  `pcall` per path so one missing sound costs one
-- failed call, and nothing else.
local function build_sounds_now()
	while qi <= #queue do
		local ok, snd = pcall(sound_object, queue[qi])
		if ok and snd then warm[#warm + 1] = snd else failed = failed + 1 end
		qi = qi + 1
	end
	sounds_done = true
	note("sounds: %s built, %s failed, synchronous at first update", #warm, failed)
end

local function finish_slicing()
	if not slicing then return end
	slicing = false
	sounds_done = true
	UnregisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", actor_on_update)
	note("sounds: %s built, %s failed, %s frames, budget %s ms/frame",
	     #warm, failed, slice_frames, BUDGET_MS)
	if VERBOSE then
		printf("[alao_prewarm %s] done", VERSION)
		for i = 1, #report do printf("[alao_prewarm]   %s", report[i]) end
	end
end

function actor_on_update()
	if not slicing then return end
	slice_frames = slice_frames + 1

	if budget_units then
		local base = timer:time()
		while qi <= #queue do
			timer:start()
			local ok, snd = pcall(sound_object, queue[qi])
			timer:stop()
			if ok and snd then warm[#warm + 1] = snd else failed = failed + 1 end
			qi = qi + 1
			if (timer:time() - base) >= budget_units then break end
		end
	else
		local n = 0
		while qi <= #queue and n < FALLBACK_BATCH do
			local ok, snd = pcall(sound_object, queue[qi])
			if ok and snd then warm[#warm + 1] = snd else failed = failed + 1 end
			qi = qi + 1
			n = n + 1
		end
	end

	if qi > #queue or slice_frames >= MAX_SLICE_FRAMES then
		finish_slicing()
	end
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- entry point
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- actor_on_first_update, not on_game_load: db.actor exists, every mod's
-- on_game_load has run (the profiler puts on_game_load at frame 6 and this at
-- frame 7), and the loading screen is still up.  It also fires again after a
-- level change, which is exactly when UIInventory:actor_on_net_destroy has set
-- ui_inventory.GUI back to nil.
function actor_on_first_update()
	if PREWARM_INVENTORY then prewarm_inventory() end
	-- after prewarm_inventory, and after FDDA / SortingPlus: this mod's script
	-- name sorts last, so its listener runs last in the actor_on_first_update
	-- pass and GUI is there to grow.
	if PREWARM_INV_POOL then prewarm_inv_pool() end
	if PREWARM_ENCYCLOPEDIA then prewarm_encyclopedia() end
	if PREWARM_TUTORIAL then prewarm_tutorial() end

	local queued = 0
	if PREWARM_SOUNDS and not sounds_done then
		queue, qi, failed, slice_frames = {}, 1, 0, 0
		build_sound_queue()
		queued = #queue
		if queued == 0 then
			note("sounds: nothing to queue")
			sounds_done = true
		elseif SLICE_SOUNDS then
			if not timer then
				units_per_ms = calibrate()
				timer = profile_timer and profile_timer() or nil
				budget_units = (timer and units_per_ms) and (BUDGET_MS * units_per_ms) or nil
			end
			slicing = true
			RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", actor_on_update)
			note("sounds: %s paths queued for slicing, timer %s",
			     queued, budget_units and "calibrated" or "uncalibrated (fixed batch)")
			return               -- the report is printed when the queue drains
		else
			build_sounds_now()
		end
	end

	if VERBOSE then
		printf("[alao_prewarm %s] done", VERSION)
		for i = 1, #report do printf("[alao_prewarm]   %s", report[i]) end
	end
	report = {}
end

function on_game_start()
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_first_update", actor_on_first_update)
end

-- Test hooks.  Nothing in the game calls these.
function _alao_state()
	return {queue = queue, qi = qi, warm = warm, failed = failed,
	        slicing = slicing, frames = slice_frames, report = report,
	        budget_units = budget_units, sounds_done = sounds_done,
	        slice_sounds = SLICE_SOUNDS}
end

-- Non-destructive: the real queue and cursor are put back afterwards, so a test
-- can ask "what would the queue be" without disturbing a run in progress.
function _alao_build_queue()
	local sq, si, sr = queue, qi, report
	queue, report = {}, {}
	build_sound_queue()
	local out = queue
	queue, qi, report = sq, si, sr
	return out
end
